Now I understand the vulnerability. The TX buffer is `maxpktsize` (1540 bytes). In `rx_icmp`:

```c
struct ip *ip = tx_ip(ifp, 1, ifp->ip, pkt->ip->src,
                      sizeof(struct icmp) + pkt->pay.len);
struct icmp *icmp = (struct icmp *) (ip + 1);
memset(icmp, 0, sizeof(*icmp));
memcpy(icmp + 1, pkt->pay.buf, pkt->pay.len);
```

The `memcpy` copies `pkt->pay.len` bytes to `icmp + 1` in the TX buffer. The TX buffer is only 1540 bytes. The `pkt->pay.len` is derived from the incoming packet's raw length. If the incoming ICMP packet has a large payload (up to the max packet size of 1540 bytes), then `pkt->pay.len` could be up to ~1500 bytes. The destination is `icmp + 1` which is at offset `sizeof(eth) + sizeof(ip) + sizeof(icmp)` = 14 + 20 + 4 = 38 bytes into the TX buffer. So copying ~1500 bytes to offset 38 would overflow the 1540-byte TX buffer.

Wait, but the incoming packet is also limited to `maxpktsize` (1540 bytes). Let me check how the raw packet length is determined. The `pkt->pay.len` is set by `mkpay`:

```c
static void mkpay(struct pkt *pkt, void *p) {
  pkt->pay = mkstr(p, (size_t) (&pkt->raw.buf[pkt->raw.len] - (uint8_t *) p));
}
```

So `pkt->pay.len` = `pkt->raw.len - offset_of_icmp_payload`. The raw packet can be up to 1540 bytes. The ICMP payload starts at offset 14 (eth) + 20 (ip) + 4 (icmp) = 38 bytes. So `pkt->pay.len` can be up to 1540 - 38 = 1502 bytes.

In `rx_icmp`, the `memcpy` writes `pkt->pay.len` bytes starting at `icmp + 1` in the TX buffer. The TX buffer offset of `icmp + 1` is 14 (eth) + 20 (ip) + 4 (icmp) = 38 bytes. So the write goes from offset 38 to 38 + 1502 = 1540, which exactly fills the TX buffer. Hmm, that doesn't overflow.

Wait, let me re-check. The `tx_ip` function writes the IP header at `eth + 1` (offset 14), and the IP header is 20 bytes. Then `icmp` is at `ip + 1` (offset 34), and `icmp + 1` is at offset 38. The `memcpy` writes `pkt->pay.len` bytes from offset 38. If `pkt->pay.len` is 1502, the write ends at offset 1540, which is exactly the end of the TX buffer. So no overflow there.

